// Goal 6 status correction and evidence summaries for SFSU IM 2025-2026.

// 6.7-ins: Initiated -> Defined
// Reason: automated UDOIT evaluation runs each term with measurable results
// (71%->80s), Canvas Bot daily scans for accommodation courses, named staff
// (Kevin Connor + 6 students) run the remediation it triggers. Consistent
// and repeatable practice. Gaps (no manual technique, no written schedule)
// keep it from Established, not Defined.
MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.7-ins-sfsu'})-[r:status_is]->(:StatusLevel)
DELETE r
WITH yse
MATCH (sl:StatusLevel {status_level: 'Defined'})
MERGE (yse)-[:status_is]->(sl);

// Evidence summaries for all Goal 6 YSEs.

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.2-ins-sfsu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "The campus course syllabus policy references DPRC but does not link to accessibility information. The campus course review and approval process refers to accessibility requirements but does not include a link to the course accessibility checklist on the page itself. JEDI PIE addresses accessible course materials as part of its disability justice certificate. No formal route exists for DPRC to notify AT that a course needs remediation; requests arrive by email. Cristian Alvarado and Christina Passmore are working out the details of a notification process, including filtering which DPRC accommodation types are relevant to digital materials. Andrew Roderick and Daniel Fontaine implement. One implementation (Public Instructions and Checklists about Digital Course Accessibility) is retired. A plan to include accessibility information in the course approval process is on hold.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.4-ins-sfsu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "AT and CEETL run multiple concurrent faculty development courses in the LMS across semesters, among them Excellence in Online Pedagogies, Renovate and Elevate in Canvas, and Universal Design for Learning. Each course has run for multiple semesters with AT and CEETL facilitators. AT's internal CPaGE processes target 85% accessibility on course shells using the built-in Canvas checker, with student assistants checking tags. AT does not push QLT or Quality Matters; Andrew Roderick sees UDL 3.0 as a stronger pedagogical basis. DPRC no longer has resources to support course accessibility reviews. Kevin Connor, Ash Klein, Daniel Fontaine, and Angie Petty implement. CPaGE practices operate within AT and are not campus-wide. A plan to verify accessibility information in AT/CEETL courses is on hold.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.5-ins-sfsu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Seven implementations address accessibility at course redesign, among them JEDI Teaching Square, the Accessible Syllabus Tool, JEDI PIE, AT's internal CPaGE processes, and DesignPLUS. DesignPLUS was piloted over summer 2026 with 12 faculty who each redesigned at least one fall course. Pilot faculty recorded showcase videos published on the DesignPLUS webpage. AT treats DesignPLUS as an emergent, roughly three-year capacity project rather than core practice. Andrew Roderick committed to producing a one-pager documenting DesignPLUS's accessibility value, but no documentation has been delivered. AT is running a longitudinal evaluation with faculty sponsor Chris Bettinger comparing DesignPLUS and non-DesignPLUS courses. The JEDI Teaching Square is co-led by Julie Paulson through a CSU Stanislaus grant focused on neurodivergence and course-redesign accessibility. CEETL funds about 8 additional participants to reach a cohort of 20. The Accessible Syllabus Tool is supported but not promoted. CPaGE practices operate within one unit. No pre- or post-redesign metrics exist. The budget element of this indicator is unmet.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.7-ins-sfsu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "AT scanned spring 2026 Canvas courses with UDOIT and raised average accessibility scores from about 71% to the 80s by summer. Batch UDOIT scans run each term, fixing missing alt text, wrong heading levels, insufficient color contrast, and decorative text styled as headings. DPRC's Canvas Bot runs daily scans for courses where a student has requested accommodation, giving alt media staff immediate access to flagged files. Cristian Alvarado and Daniel Fontaine implement, and Kevin Connor with six student workers staff the remediation the evaluations trigger. No manual evaluation method exists. No written scan schedule is on record. SF State's longstanding accommodation-triggered course review, where enrollment by a student with an accommodation triggers an accessibility review, is not currently operating.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.8-ins-sfsu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Kevin Connor runs day-to-day Canvas remediation with the work in his position description. Six student workers help, three doing most of the work at the help desk between customers. AT inherited Canvas remediation in spring 2026 and runs batch UDOIT scans, random student-designer assignment, mail-merge faculty notifications, and rescans on completion. The spreadsheet of record feeds per-department reports for chairs and deans. Spring 2026 covered about 4,000 courses and summer 862. By October 2026, 21.7% of ATI points had been removed. Post-remediation UDOIT scores trend in the mid-to-high 80s. PDF remediation has run on test courses only. The Accessible Media Quick Converter handles format conversion. AT is pursuing the CSUEB AI document remediation platform at the SFBRN level rather than hosting it at SF State alone. Whiteboard images and math formulas have no remediation path. A plan to maintain a shared ATI materials repository is on hold.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.9-ins-sfsu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "One TAAP exists (Pearson Revel). The SFBRN CSUBuy IT Accessibility Review Procedure operates with documented cross-campus queue rules, a 30-day pickup SLA for unassigned reviews, reviewer triage heuristics, standard communication templates, and a rule of three for unresponsive requesters. People are directed to BuyIT for LTI accessibility review, but no campus policy requires publisher tools to go through BuyIT. Faculty request publisher tools through the bookstore, which bypasses the review entirely. The retired SF State Accessible Technology Procurement Process has no active replacement. No Equally Effective Alternate Access Plan exists for the LMS or its integrations. A plan for campus-wide TAAP implementation including ITS, DPRC, HR, and Colleges is not started. A plan for clearer EEAAP instructions for LTI is on hold.";
